  "measurement_script_sha256": "6aeb137d88597e7dfff49df23c9abf9d0f244ad3e21c9ba402eb1a6442319e47",
  "assessment": "Descriptive workflow comparison: scans048–052 before; scans054–058 after.\nAll ten pages are committed; the original common baseline matches the recomputation.\n\nMeasure | Before | After\nMarks | 65 | 106\nSum of accepted page windows, seconds | 1470.115734 | 2195.727849\nMarks per summed accepted-page-window hour | 159.17 | 173.79\nSum of individual primary reader intervals, seconds | 1741.692546 | 3306.978040\nMarks per summed individual-primary-reader hour | 134.35 | 115.39\nPrimary crop views | 118 | 188\nPrimary page views | 11 | 14\nPrimary crops per mark | 1.815 | 1.774\nDecisions before: 24 first, 1 alternative, 2 other, 38 undecided.\nDecisions after: 37 first, 3 alternative, 2 other, 64 undecided.\n\nDescriptive relative changes: accepted-window rate +9.2%; individual-primary-interval rate -14.1%; crops per mark -2.3%.\nAccepted page windows run from each page's earliest primary start to its commit; their sum omits interpage dispatch gaps. Individual primary intervals run from each reader's recorded start to final own-review finish, include waiting and revision gaps, and are not continuous active-reading or CPU time. Overlapping readers' intervals are summed separately. These individual-reader sums differ from the page primary spans used in some earlier findings. Rates include undecided marks; view counts are primary views, with independent-peer views excluded.\nThe documented direct cases remain: M1026 crop metadata correction used 0 new ink views and changed 0 readings; M1051's own revision took 12 seconds and 1 new view, broadened uncertainty, and settled 0 additional marks.\nAfter-cohort results are descriptive. Different page content and mark difficulty, reader allocation, peer questions and revision gaps prevent attributing the differences to the reminders. Decision counts are not an accuracy measurement or a count of newly settled marks. Both reminders clarify existing inspection requirements."
